# **The rails, in two parts, and both heights were measured rather than picked.**
#
# A metre of solid bulwark (1.40 to 2.40) and a thin cap bar floating above it
# (2.68 to 2.92), with a 0.28 m gap between. It began as one solid metre and
# `assets/test/rig_walk.loom` walked its pilot straight over the east one: a hop
# at `deeper_player.rhai`'s numbers puts the capsule's feet at 2.733, so a 1.0 m
# rail is something you clear. The run ended at y = -974 and still falling,
# because nothing in this engine catches a character in water.
#
# Raising it to one solid 1.4 m wall did **not** fix it, and that is the
# interesting half: feet at 2.733 against a cap at 2.80 is clear by seven
# centimetres, and the controller stepped the capsule up onto the cap anyway,
# because `step_height` applies in the air as readily as on a stair. Both
# numbers had to move — the cap out of reach, and the character's step down to
# 0.10.
#
# It is a bar rather than a taller wall because a 1.5 m wall **hid the boat**.
# The player's eye is at 3.05 and the thing this hub exists to point at is a
# hull floating 0.9 m off the edge; a parapet the height of a man turns that
# into a superstructure behind a fence. The gap sees the water, the bar stops
# the jump, and a capsule 1.8 m tall always spans the bar's band whatever part
# of a hop it is in — that last clause is why the gap is safe rather than a
# second hole.
#
# **Closed on the berth side too, for now.** The way aboard is the round that
# builds a gangway; until then a gap in this railing is a hole a player falls
# through into an unrecoverable state, and a demo must not have one. The two
# bollards in front of it are what say "berth" instead.

[[node]]
name = "RailSouth"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 1.90, 6.90], scale = [12.0, 0.50, 0.10] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.30, 0.21, 0.16]
  roughness = 0.85

[[node]]
name = "RailWest"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [-11.90, 1.90, 0.0], scale = [0.10, 0.50, 7.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.30, 0.21, 0.16]
  roughness = 0.85

# **Split for the slipway: 2.8 m of gap at z = +2.0 to +4.8.**
#
# Off-centre, and that is not arbitrary. `assets/test/rig_walk.loom`'s pilot
# crosses the deck along z = +0.90 with its lateral velocity pinned to zero, so
# a gap on the centreline is a gap it walks straight out of — and its
# `Player.y > 2.2` assertion, which means "still on the deck", would then be
# failed by the pilot doing something perfectly correct. The gap is a pace
# south of that lane instead.

[[node]]
name = "RailEastNorth"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [11.90, 1.90, -2.50], scale = [0.10, 0.50, 4.50] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.30, 0.21, 0.16]
  roughness = 0.85

[[node]]
name = "RailEastSouth"
parent = "Rig"
transform = { pos = [11.90, 1.90, 5.90], scale = [0.10, 0.50, 1.10] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.30, 0.21, 0.16]
  roughness = 0.85

# **The berth side is open between the bollards, and that is the way aboard.**
# 3.8 m of gap at x -6.9..-3.1, which is the two bollards' own span plus a pace
# either side. Round 1 closed it because a hole in the railing was an
# unrecoverable fall; the slipway at the east end is what makes falling in
# recoverable, and it had to exist before this could be opened.

# **And split again in round 6, for the ladder.** 2.0 m of gap at x -10.30 to
# -8.30 — the far west of the berth side, clear of the boat's own transom at
# x = -6.5. See `Ladder` below for the measured reason it had to exist: the
# slipway at the east end is the only way out of the water and it is twenty-six
# metres from where the taught gesture drops a player who misses to port.

